    "## Recommended Learning Order\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Basics (01-02)**: linear regression builds regression evaluation and gradient descent; logistic regression extends linear models to probabilistic classification - both share the same \"loss -> gradient -> update\" paradigm.\n",
    "2. **Classic classification (03-06)**: decision trees, naive Bayes, SVM, and KNN represent four different inductive biases: partition rules, probabilistic inference, margin maximization, and local memory.\n",
    "3. **Unsupervised & comprehensive (07-08)**: K-Means groups unlabeled data; the credit-card case puts five single models into a unified evaluation framework, comparing cross-validation, thresholds, calibration, and feature importance.\n",
    "4. **Ensembles (09)**: Random Forest (Bagging) and GBDT/XGBoost (Boosting) are the workhorses for tabular data - note the difference between parallel variance reduction and sequential bias reduction.\n",
    "5. **Advanced topics (10-11)**: PCA handles high-dimensional redundancy, speed, and visualization; kernel methods generalize the SVM kernel trick from chapter 05 to ridge regression and PCA.\n",
    "6. **Engineering & projects (12-17)**: feature engineering and tuning complete the engineering chain; chapter 13 benchmarks 16 models (3 unsupervised + 9 classification + 4 regression) on iris under one scale; chapter 14 applies the skeleton to Bitcoin time series, with regression as the main thread and a \"return vs volatility\" comparison; chapter 15 applies it to Fashion-MNIST images, covering high-dimensional pixel handling and error analysis; chapter 16 completes a quant + ML project (cross-sectional factors -> all-model benchmark -> backtesting loop); chapter 17 completes text classification (TF-IDF -> all-model benchmark -> spam-word diagnosis). Together they cover five data modalities: tabular / time series / image / finance / text. Further quant extensions (stock pools, deeper factor research, portfolio optimization) can be continued in a separate quant research project.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Three Chapter Templates\n",
    "\n",
    "**Template A: model chapters (01-11)** - model theory -> NumPy implementation from scratch -> sklearn comparison & real cases -> chapter summary with validation results.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Template B: engineering chapter (12)** - workflow overview -> step-by-step demo -> end-to-end walkthrough -> summary; no mathematical derivations or from-scratch implementations, staying close to real engineering practice.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Template C: project chapters (13-17)** - shared skeleton: **data -> exploration (unsupervised first) -> feature engineering -> all-model benchmark -> diagnosis -> conclusion**, adapted to each data modality:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Project chapter | Exploration | Feature Engineering | Diagnosis |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| 13 tabular | PCA / kernel PCA / K-Means | standardization + derived features | decision boundaries / confusion matrix |\n",
    "| 14 time series | volatility clustering | lags + rolling features | residuals + comparison (return vs volatility) |\n",
    "| 15 image | PCA projection / image reconstruction | standardization + PCA reduction | error samples + reduction comparison |\n",
    "| 16 financial panel | K-Means market states / PCA market factors | cross-sectional factors + cross-sectional standardization | **backtest**: equity curve / drawdown / monthly heatmap |\n",
    "| 17 text | word frequency / word cloud | TF-IDF (unigrams + bigrams) | error samples / spam-word diagnosis |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Standard case deliverables**: an independent test set (or out-of-sample validation), a metrics table, and visualizations. Classification focuses on confusion matrices and ROC; regression on true-vs-predicted values, residuals, and R-squared; clustering on cluster structure and parameter selection (elbow method, silhouette score); ensembles and tree models on feature importance; dimensionality reduction on explained variance, reconstruction error, and downstream performance; quant on IC, AUC, equity curve, and drawdown."
